insert into public.questions (id, test_id, prompt, options, position, passage) values
  ('ba118573-5bea-546f-9bf9-3b0787824d1c', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'As used in the text, what does the word "specific" most nearly mean?', '["Imaginary", "Correct", "Energetic", "Particular"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The following text is from Mark Oshiro’s 2018 novel Anger Is a Gift. In the novel, Moss and his friends are on a subway train in Northern California.

Lights from the outside world then filled the train car as it rose out of the ground and climbed the elevated track. As long as Moss had lived in West Oakland, he''d never tired of this <u>specific</u> view, so he pointed toward the windows. “Check it,” he said, and the Port of Oakland began to pass by them.'),
  ('6cf57c2d-013d-5f09-838d-1536cf53ff14', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'As used in the text, what does the word \"supported\" most nearly mean?', '["Cherished", "Defended", "Held up", "Improved on"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The following text is adapted from John Matheus’s 1925 short story \"Fog.\"

The fog extended its tentacles over city and river, gradually obliterating traces of familiar landscapes. At five-thirty the old Panhandle bridge, **<u>supported**</u> by massive sandstone pillars, stalwart, as when erected fifty years before to serve a generation now passed behind the portals of life, had become a spectral outline against the sky.'),
  ('6b0dcba5-d128-50f4-9d3a-329ce05f7597', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["replicate", "import", "suppress", "critique"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Although the government of the Soviet Union attempted to _____ Georgi Vladimov’s novel Faithful Ruslan, copies of the book circulated in secret among readers in several parts of the country.'),
  ('982dd91a-b0d0-5862-9755-3353d6d4cb2d', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["credible", "heterogeneous", "imperative", "decisive"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Although the clustering of information technology firms in Northern California is often cited as typical of industrial agglomeration, Giulia Faggio et al. use UK data to show that the mix of factors driving the phenomenon is ___ across industries: while the potential for knowledge spillovers can prompt agglomeration, collocation among tube manufacturers occurs for different reasons.'),
  ('515b22be-9199-5b24-9c0e-2236bb6b1d37', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice best describes the function of the reference to the "wooing kestrel" (a type of bird) in the text as a whole?', '["It emphasizes the speaker’s belief that her friend is not sharing his true feelings with her.", "It suggests that nature has secrets that humans are fundamentally incapable of discovering.", "It illustrates the assertion that peace is a subjective state rather than an objective one.", "It presents the natural world as aligning with the experience of the speaker and her friend."]'::jsonb, 5, 'The following text is from Anne Spencer’s 1922 poem "Translation."

We trekked into a far country,
My friend and I.
Our deeper content was never spoken,
But each knew all the other said.
He told me how calm his soul was laid
By the lack of anvil and strife.
"The <u>wooing kestrel</u>," I said, "mutes his mating-note
To please the harmony of this sweet silence."'),
  ('fb4e38a8-4523-5c58-aa0e-81d29df7b966', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?', '["It predicts that non-Indigenous authors will include more Indigenous characters in their work.", "It challenges the widely held view that Stephen Graham Jones based My Heart Is a Chainsaw on events in his own life.", "It highlights the significance of the portrayal of Indigenous people in My Heart Is a Chainsaw.", "It asks why so many readers who don’t enjoy the horror genre have purchased My Heart Is a Chainsaw."]'::jsonb, 6, 'The 2021 novel My Heart Is a Chainsaw confirmed that Stephen Graham Jones is one of the most talented writers of horror fiction today. <u>By featuring main characters who are Blackfeet, like Jones</u> <u>himself, the novel also helped to ensure that Indigenous people have a place within the horror genre.</u> But Jones is hardly the only Indigenous voice in horror: Lisa Fuller, a member of the Murri people of Australia, has also written in the genre. Her acclaimed 2019 young adult novel Ghost Bird is set in a Murri community in Queensland, Australia.'),
  ('ac3327ff-68d6-57ed-9460-712316cf3941', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?', '["It suggests that a commonly held belief regarding an organism is mistaken.", "It summarizes the history of research into a biological phenomenon.", "It illustrates why one organism may pose more of a threat than another.", "It explains a practical benefit of researching a particular biological process."]'::jsonb, 7, 'Horizontal gene transfer involves the exchange of genetic material between organisms not in a parent-offspring relationship. It is common among prokaryotes (single-celled organisms, such as the bacteria Enterococcus cecorum and Moraxella caviae). <u>The process can have the effect of increasing bacterial</u> <u>resistance to antibiotics; as such, an understanding of how to prevent horizontal gene transfer might</u> <u>result in the mitigation of dangerous pathogens (organisms that cause disease).</u>'),
  ('07a42b2f-da31-5ec5-82fb-5fe6a7a72fbb', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice best states the main topic of the text?', '["**A** A possible shortcoming of a study involving Pomacentrus amboinensis", "**B** A discovery regarding the evolution of Pomacentrus amboinensis", "**C** A change in fish behavior first reported in 2022", "**D** A newly identified cause of ocean acidification"]'::jsonb, 8, 'In 2013 Oona M. Lönnstedt and colleagues published a study concluding that ocean acidification has a strong effect on the behavior of Pomacentrus amboinensis, a species of fish. However, Lönnstedt and colleagues'' study relied on a mean sample size of only about 28 fish. In a 2022 review of various scientists'' conclusions about the impacts of ocean acidification on fish behavior, Jeff C. Clements and colleagues caution that relying on such a relatively small sample size can increase the potential for biased analysis. Such analysis, in turn, can contribute to reports of exaggerated effects.'),
  ('e3100365-4fba-549d-898e-1caea1ed19fb', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'According to the text, what is a difference between how historians view Lestek and how they view Wladyslaw I Herman?', '["Historians believe that the Gesta principum Polonorum provides more evidence for Lestek’s existence than it does for Wladyslaw I Herman’s existence.", "Historians agree that Wladyslaw I Herman existed, but disagree about whether Lestek existed.", "Historians agree that Lestek ruled Poland much later than Wladyslaw I Herman.", "Historians find the orally transmitted stories affirming the existence of Wladyslaw I Herman to be more convincing than similar stories about Lestek."]'::jsonb, 9, 'Historians point to the rule of the Piast dynasty as crucial to the formation of the Polish state. However, some differentiate between members of the dynasty like Wladyslaw I Herman, who ruled as duke from 1079 to 1102 CE, and less well-documented figures like Lestek, who is said to have ruled in the 9th and 10th centuries but whose historical actuality is disputed. Lestek appears in the Gesta principum Polonorum, a chronicle of medieval Polish history written between 1112 and 1118. However, the chronicle’s documentation of Lestek relies on oral tradition, unlike its records of later rulers.'),
  ('4169bc3c-6a92-54c3-93b8-b5f51342ba0a', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice best describes data in the table that support the researcher’s claim?', '["Nackten is at a higher latitude than Lake Kegonsa and typically had fewer days of ice per winter than Lake Kegonsa did.", "Spirit Lake had 102 days of ice in the winter of 1980-81 and 126 days of ice in the winter of 2005- 06.", "Nackten had 177 days of ice in the winter of 1980-81 and 134 days of ice in the winter of 2005- 06.", "Nackten is at a higher latitude than Lake Kegonsa and typically had more days of ice per winter than Lake Kegonsa did."]'::jsonb, 10, '**Days per Winter that Lakes Have Surface Ice**

| Lake | Latitude (degrees) | 1980- 81 | 1985- 86 | 1990- 91 | 1995- 96 | 2000- 01 | 2005- 06 |
| Spirit Lake | 43.46 | 102 | 135 | 121 | 134 | 147 | 126 |
| Lake Kegonsa | 42.97 | 94 | 116 | 104 | 113 | 124 | 101 |
| Nackten | 62.913 | 177 | 168 | 144 | 174 | 133 | 134 |

It is common for freshwater lakes near or above a latitude of 45° north of the equator, like Lake Stechlin in Germany, to accumulate surface ice in winter. The amount and duration of ice depends on many factors, including local weather conditions as well as the lake’s depth, volume, and surface area, but a climate researcher claims that the higher a lake’s latitude, the more days per winter it will typically have measurable amounts of surface ice.'),
  ('e5858a74-1b36-5997-874d-21e469d231ad', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the comparison?', '["above all three of the other industries listed in the table.", "above either administration/waste or construction and nearly equal to\ntransportation/warehousing.", "below all three of the other industries listed in the table.", "below transportation/warehousing but above both construction and administration/waste."]'::jsonb, 11, '**Impact of Four Key Industries on Oklahoma Economy in 2017**

| Industry | Approximately total contribution by industry | The number of people employed by industry | Average contribution per employee by industry |
| Administration/waste | \$5,830,600,000 | 95,964 | \$60,132 |
| Construction | \$6,797,300,000 | 77,247 | \$87,994 |
| Transportation/ warehousing | \$12,414,600,000 | 52,891 | \$234,720 |
| Tribal economic activity | \$7,312,400,000 | 51,674 | \$143,510 |

The Cherokee Nation, the Quapaw Tribe, and the more than thirty other tribes in Oklahoma operate numerous businesses and generate billions of dollars in revenue. An economics student is researching the tribes'' collective activity as a single industry. The student wants to compare the average amount that industry contributed per employee to Oklahoma’s economy with the average amount contributed per employee by three other industries. Looking at the table, the student finds that tribal economic activity contributed over \$141,000 per employee, on average, ranking it _____'),
  ('e6dba369-4e06-54c9-9f23-81b28bae5a8b', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which quotation from a translation of Uncle Vanya most effectively illustrates the claim?', '["Vanya says to Professor Serebrakoff, “For twenty-five years I have managed [the country estate], and have sent you the returns from it like the most honest of servants, and you have never given me one single word of thanks for my work, not one— neither in my youth nor now.”", "Professor Serebrakoff says to Helena, “It is funny that everybody listens to [Vanya] and his old idiot of a mother, but the moment I open my lips you all begin to feel ill-treated.”", "Vanya says to Professor Serebrakoff, \"This place [the country estate] could never have been bought had I not renounced my inheritance in favor of my sister [the professor’s late wife], whom I deeply loved-and what is more, I worked for ten years like an ox, and paid off the debt.\"", "Vanya says to himself, “I was proud of [Professor Serebrakoff] and of his learning; I received all his words and writings as inspired and now? Now he has retired, and what is the total of his life? A blank! He is absolutely unknown, and his fame has burst like a soap-bubble. I have been deceived; I see that now, basely deceived.\""]'::jsonb, 12, 'Uncle Vanya: Scenes from Country Life in Four Acts is an 1898 play by Anton Chekhov, originally written in Russian. In the play, Professor Serebrakoff and his wife Helena have come to stay at their country estate, which Vanya manages with the help of Sonia, the professor’s daughter from a previous marriage. Chekhov depicts Vanya’s opinion of Professor Serebrakoff as having changed significantly, as is evident when____'),
  ('23918ef1-2175-5826-8388-4a98325767da', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice best describes data in the graph that support the researchers'' conclusion?', '["The managers who reflected on a past mistake that resulted in learning exhibited more humility on average than the managers in the other two groups did.", "None of the three groups'' average humility scores exceeded 3.5.", "The managers in the control group exhibited only slightly less humility on average than the managers in the two experimental groups did.", "All three groups exhibited less humility on average than the researchers expected."]'::jsonb, 13, '![figure](/q/may24/001.webp)

Jia Hu and colleagues hypothesized that workplace leaders who reflect on lessons learned from past mistakes are likely to exhibit more humility than leaders who don’t engage in such reflection. To test this, the team placed 301 managers in one of three groups. Participants in two experimental groups were asked to reflect on a mistake, one group focusing on a mistake that resulted in learning and the other group focusing on a mistake that didn’t result in learning. Participants in a control group were asked to reflect on their daily routine. All the participants then described how they would respond to a workplace scenario. After evaluating the responses for evidence of humility, the researchers concluded that their hypothesis was correct.'),
  ('69b39809-03b9-5396-a33e-89c58e761bca', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["overemphasizing the extent to which Ephron took inspiration from earlier writers.", "positing unsupportable connections between Heartburn and Ephron’s life.", "misrepresenting Heartburn as being more widely read than it actually is.", "minimizing the fact that Ephron drew on real-world material when writing Heartburn."]'::jsonb, 14, 'Nora Ephron’s 1983 novel Heartburn is regularly described as autobiographical. That characterization is apt—there are many parallels between the experiences of the novel’s narrator, Rachel Samstat, and those of Ephron—but it should not be taken to mean that all the people and events depicted in Heartburn are based on actual people and events. The novel is largely pure invention, and readers who neglect this fact and instead try to identify more and more real-life analogues thus risk__'),
  ('f7f0e754-175e-5f1f-abd1-fc8f6401e69e', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["is", "are being", "have been", "are"]'::jsonb, 15, 'What is the correct pronunciation of Kiribati? In the Gilbertese language spoken by residents of the island nation, the letter combination -ti makes the -s sound; as a result, the country’s name ___ pronounced ''Kiribas''.'),
  ('8becb0ac-b4b9-53b2-bbc3-4d4b489bb2a0', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["to study", "will study", "who study", "studying"]'::jsonb, 16, 'A recipient of both the Wolf Prize in Chemistry and the Robert A. Welch Award, chemist Ahmed Zewail expanded the limits of measurability when he devised a novel technique in optical spectroscopy that allows researchers___chemical reactions occurring within molecules at a quadrillionth of a second, or one femtosecond.'),
  ('f9f8679d-d093-5887-8d3a-0501047e68d6', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["town", "town''s", "towns", "towns''"]'::jsonb, 17, 'Both Arteaga, in the state of Coahuila, and Lagos de Moreno, in the state of Jalisco, have been designated by Mexico as pueblos mágicos (magical villages) to celebrate these ______ natural beauty and unique cultural traditions.'),
  ('fed98fd6-4c70-59a3-90a2-03dead4b858d', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["using", "to use", "use", "used"]'::jsonb, 18, 'The size of bird eggs varies widely across species, and the researchers,___computer imaging to compare the lengths of eggs from species like Chloroceryle americana and Francolinus coqui, aimed to shed light on the evolutionary basis for such variation.'),
  ('57872dd8-9d2a-5f5e-9e75-97c19f842c3a', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["lists: in", "lists in", "lists in:", "lists, in"]'::jsonb, 19, 'A lady-in-waiting’s account of Japanese courtly life in the tenth century, Sei Shōnagon’s Pillow Book is filled with daily observations and musings. Some of these appear as ___ “Things That Arouse a Fond Memory of the Past,” Shōnagon catalogs her appreciation for pressed silk, a moonlit night, and dried hollyhock.'),
  ('ffdfb398-7460-5229-904e-8d3513c2da81', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["artist, and 2007 Eiteljorg Fellowship recipient", "artist and 2007 Eiteljorg Fellowship recipient", "artist and 2007 Eiteljorg Fellowship recipient-", "artist and 2007 Eiteljorg Fellowship recipient,"]'::jsonb, 20, 'In his work, acclaimed Cahuilla___Gerald Clarke uses basketry, acrylic paintings, and other mediums to explore Native perspectives on truth and beauty.'),
  ('8527fba7-a7ea-52d4-a145-e5c69a77997c', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["For example,", "Finally,", "Similarly,", "Therefore,"]'::jsonb, 21, 'Scientists long debated the origins of chondrules, tiny glass beads that formed in meteors billions of years ago; for decades, different theories were proposed, from lightning strikes to powerful rock collisions, but none had sufficient evidentiary support. ___ scientists found strong evidence that chondrules were formed by shock waves in nearby nebulae.'),
  ('bae44aa8-5b18-5512-b9f4-e0f1350f1764', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["In contrast,", "Accordingly,", "Meanwhile,", "Nevertheless,"]'::jsonb, 22, 'Voting members of the 2006 Latin Grammys were impressed by Los Tigres del Norte’s album Historias Que Contar and its contribution to the norteño genre, a form of regional Mexican music featuring accordion and bajo sexto guitar that first developed in northern Mexico in the late nineteenth century. ___ they awarded the group the Best Norteño Album award.'),
  ('328fef0a-1fe1-5f56-ada5-4e9d2d9f0ce6', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Conversely,", "Likewise,", "Regardless,", "Therefore,"]'::jsonb, 23, 'As anthropologist Cristina Grasseni explains, with the rise in demand for artisanal cheeses claiming to be typical of specific Italian regions, such as Pecorino Siciliano cheese from Sicily, came the need to guarantee the veracity of such claims to consumers. ___ the European Union established DOP status, a label certifying that the cheese was made according to the region’s local traditions.'),
  ('3f7f04d5-e494-5e3d-9750-97d793fbad65', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'The student wants to emphasize a difference between the two species of foxes. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Genes, which are in fennec fox chromosomes, carry critical instructions for determining the fox’s physical traits.", "While both are species of foxes, the fennec fox and the red fox don’t have the same number of chromosomes: fennec foxes have sixty-four, whereas red foxes have only thirty-four.", "The fennec fox and the red fox both have chromosomes, which contain genes.", "The red fox, a species of fox, has thirty-four cellular structures called chromosomes."]'::jsonb, 24, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Chromosomes are cellular structures that contain genes.
Genes carry critical instructions for determining an organism’s physical traits.
The fennec fox and the red fox are both species of foxes.
As a species, the fennec fox has sixty-four chromosomes.
As a species, the red fox has thirty-four chromosomes.'),
  ('9514f633-298f-5bbb-95fb-4533e4b480da', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'The student wants to compare the causes of crown shyness proposed in the two theories. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Ng posits the mutual shade avoidance theory, whereas Jacobs proposes an alternative theory.", "Both Jacobs and Ng have proposed theories to explain what causes crown shyness.", "While Jacobs proposes that crown shyness is caused by neighboring tree branches brushing against one another, Ng posits that it occurs when branches detect shade from nearby trees'' branches.", "Jacobs’s mutual abrasion theory proposes that when neighboring trees brush against one another, branches break off, resulting in a phenomenon in which the tops of trees grow close together but don’t overlap."]'::jsonb, 25, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Crown shyness is a phenomenon in which the tops (crowns) of neighboring trees grow close together but don’t overlap.
To explain how this happens, Australian forester M.R. Jacobs proposes the mutual abrasion theory. According to Jacobs’s theory, when trees brush against one another, branches break off. Malaysian scholar Francis S.P. Ng posits the mutual shade avoidance theory.
According to Ng’s theory, when tree branches detect shade from nearby trees’ branches, they stop growing.'),
  ('e60d6998-8e4e-5f7e-ae8f-b58727178e28', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to emphasize a difference between the eggs of the two species?', '["A 2017 study compared the lengths of eggs produced by an array of different bird species, such as Acrocephalus scirpaceus and Alcedo atthis.", "The bird species Acrocephalus scirpaceus, which belongs to the order Passeriformes, and Alcedo atthis, of the order Coraciiformes, were included in a 2017 study that compared the average lengths of their eggs.", "Mary Caswell Stoddard led a research study that determined the average lengths of eggs, including those of Acrocephalus scirpaceus birds (1.80 cm) and Alcedo atthis birds (2.19 cm).", "A 2017 study found that Acrocephalus scirpaceus eggs had an average length of 1.80 cm, whereas Alcedo atthis eggs were longer, with an average length of 2.19 cm."]'::jsonb, 26, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

In 2017, a research team led by Mary Caswell Stoddard determined the average lengths of eggs produced by various bird species.
Acrocephalus scirpaceus is a species of bird in the order Passeriformes.
Acrocephalus scirpaceus eggs had an average length of 1.80 cm.
Alcedo atthis is a species of bird in the order Coraciiformes.
Alcedo atthis eggs had an average length of 2.19 cm.'),
  ('e864f04e-4899-5442-8fe0-2f12274ea8e5', '6d75c504-f1fa-5e17-a3ff-f4c0d7fb511e', 'Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to emphasize a difference between Vickers and Crosfield?', '["Though it was conducted long ago, the research performed by algologist Anna Vickers and geologist Margaret Crosfield can help us understand how scientific knowledge evolves.", "Algology and geology are just two examples of the myriad fields in which scientists can specialize.", "Anna Vickers studied algae in the Canary Islands; Margaret Crosfield, however, studied the hills of Northeast Wales.", "Both Anna Vickers and Margaret Crosfield are known for their contributions to science."]'::jsonb, 27, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Understanding the history of science is critical for understanding how knowledge evolves. Anna Vickers was a French algologist born in 1852.
She is known for her research on algae in the Canary Islands.
Margaret Crosfield was a British geologist born in 1859.
She is known for her research on the hills of Northeast Wales.')
on conflict (id) do update set test_id = excluded.test_id, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options,
  position = excluded.position, passage = excluded.passage;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct_index, correct_answers, explanation) values
  ('ba118573-5bea-546f-9bf9-3b0787824d1c', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** In the sentence, “this specific view” points to a distinct, particular view that Moss recognizes and appreciates every time the train rises. The context (“so he pointed toward the windows. ‘Check it,’ he said, and the Port of Oakland began to pass by them.”) identifies a concrete, definite scene—meaning “specific” = particular.
**Choice A is incorrect.** “Imaginary” would mean the view is imagined or not real, but the passage describes an actual view outside the train, not something invented by Moss.
**Choice B is incorrect.** “Correct” doesn’t match the sense here; the sentence isn’t evaluating truth or accuracy but naming a particular view.
**Choice C is incorrect.** “Energetic” refers to liveliness or activity; the phrase “specific view” refers to identity or distinctness, not energy.
**Faster approach:** Substitute each choice into the phrase “this ___ view” and choose the word that naturally describes a distinct, identifiable scene—“particular” fits immediately.'),
  ('6cf57c2d-013d-5f09-838d-1536cf53ff14', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** In the context the bridge is described with "massive sandstone pillars," which are structural elements that physically bear the weight of the bridge. The surrounding imagery (a spectral outline against the sky) emphasizes the bridge’s physical presence rather than an emotional or abstract sense of support, so "held up" matches the literal, physical meaning.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Cherished" suggests an emotional attachment, which is not supported by the physical description of pillars and structure in the passage.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Defended" implies protection from attack or harm; the passage refers to the bridge’s physical foundations, not any protecting action.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Improved on" would mean altered or made better; nothing in the sentence indicates renovation or enhancement—only that the pillars bear the bridge.
**Faster approach:** For words in context, identify nearby concrete nouns (here, "pillars") to decide if the word is literal (physical) or figurative; if physical elements are present, choose the literal meaning.'),
  ('6b0dcba5-d128-50f4-9d3a-329ce05f7597', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The phrase "copies of the book circulated in secret" indicates that the government tried to prevent the book’s distribution; to "suppress" means to stop or restrain something from being published or distributed, which directly explains why copies would have to circulate secretly.
**Choice A is incorrect.** To "replicate" means to copy or reproduce; if the government were trying to replicate the novel, that would not explain secret circulation and is inconsistent with the sentence.
**Choice B is incorrect.** To "import" means to bring something into a country; that does not match the context of a government acting against a work or the need for secrecy.
**Choice D is incorrect.** To "critique" means to analyze or evaluate; while a government might critique a work, that would not by itself explain why copies circulated in secret as if distribution were being prevented.
**Faster approach:** Look at the clause "circulated in secret" and pick the choice that most directly explains why secrecy was necessary—select the word meaning to prevent publication or distribution (suppress).'),
  ('982dd91a-b0d0-5862-9755-3353d6d4cb2d', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence is contrasting the reasons for agglomeration across different industries, so it needs a word meaning "varying" or "different." "Heterogeneous" precisely conveys that the mix of factors differs by industry—knowledge spillovers explain some clusters, while other industries (like tube manufacturers) cluster for other reasons.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Credible" means believable, which does not make sense here: the sentence is about variation in causal factors, not about believability.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Imperative" means essential or necessary; that meaning conflicts with the following clause, which describes different motivating factors rather than something being required.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Decisive" means conclusive or determinative; it would imply a single overriding cause, contradicting the sentence’s contrast that different industries cluster for different reasons.
**Faster approach:** Look for a word that signals "varied/different" to fit the contrast; eliminate choices that mean "believable," "necessary," or "conclusive," leaving "heterogeneous."'),
  ('515b22be-9199-5b24-9c0e-2236bb6b1d37', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The speaker’s image of the "wooing kestrel" muting its mating-note to "please the harmony of this sweet silence" shows that the natural world is behaving in the same way as the speaker and her friend: it is holding back sound to preserve calm. The kestrel’s action mirrors the pair’s unspoken understanding and the preserved quiet, so the reference presents nature as aligning with their experience.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The kestrel’s muting suggests concurrence with silence, not that the friend is concealing feelings; the friend explicitly describes his calm, and the bird image reinforces shared stillness rather than distrust.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The line attributes deliberate behavior to the kestrel (it "mutes" to please the harmony), not an inscrutable secret beyond human reach.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The image emphasizes shared, observable silence rather than arguing that peace is merely subjective; it shows an external natural scene matching their internal state.
**Faster approach:** For questions about a figurative reference, first read the literal image, then ask whether it parallels, contrasts, or complicates the speakers’ situation — that relationship identifies the answer quickly.'),
  ('fb4e38a8-4523-5c58-aa0e-81d29df7b966', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The underlined sentence explains the novel’s effect: because it features Blackfeet main characters, it "helped to ensure that Indigenous people have a place within the horror genre," which highlights the significance of that portrayal. The sentence is evaluative and asserts cultural importance, so its function is to emphasize the novel’s meaningful representation of Indigenous people.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The sentence does not make a prediction about what non-Indigenous authors will do in the future; it comments on the novel’s impact rather than forecasting others’ behavior.
**Choice B is incorrect.** There is no challenge to biographical claims or suggestion that the novel is based on Jones’s life; the sentence notes that Jones is Blackfeet and that the novel’s characters are too, not that it was derived from his personal events.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The sentence does not ask a question about readers or sales, nor does it express puzzlement about readers who dislike horror; it asserts the novel’s role in representation.
**Faster approach:** For questions about a sentence’s function, look for signal words (e.g., "helped," "ensured," "however") to decide whether the sentence explains, emphasizes, predicts, questions, or contrasts—then match that function to the answer choices.'),
  ('ac3327ff-68d6-57ed-9460-712316cf3941', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The underlined sentence draws a direct practical consequence from the description of horizontal gene transfer: because the process can increase antibiotic resistance, understanding how to prevent it could help mitigate dangerous pathogens. That is an explicit statement of a practical benefit of researching the biological process.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The sentence does not challenge a commonly held belief about an organism; it states a consequence and possible application of studying the process.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The sentence does not summarize research history; it proposes a benefit of future understanding, not a chronological account of past studies.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The sentence does not compare threat levels of different organisms or illustrate why one organism is more dangerous than another; it speaks generally about reducing pathogen risk by preventing gene transfer.
**Faster approach:** Look for signal words (e.g., "as such," "thus," "therefore") to identify sentences that state conclusions or practical implications; such sentences typically express the passage’s purpose or benefit, pointing to answers like D.'),
  ('07a42b2f-da31-5ec5-82fb-5fe6a7a72fbb', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage focuses on a potential problem with Lönnstedt and colleagues’ 2013 study — namely its small mean sample size (about 28 fish) — and reports that a 2022 review warns such small samples can produce biased analyses and exaggerated effects. The central idea is therefore a possible shortcoming in that study.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage says nothing about evolution; it discusses study methods and sample size, not evolutionary findings about Pomacentrus amboinensis.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage does not report a new 2022 observation of changed fish behavior; instead it reports a 2022 review criticizing earlier methodology.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage refers to ocean acidification as a phenomenon under study but does not identify any new cause of ocean acidification.
**Faster approach:** Look for the author’s main action word (here, “caution”/“criticize”) to identify whether the passage presents a finding or a critique; if it’s a critique of methods, choose the option about a shortcoming.'),
  ('e3100365-4fba-549d-898e-1caea1ed19fb', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage states that Wladyslaw I Herman "ruled as duke from 1079 to 1102 CE," presenting him as an established historical figure, while Lestek is described as someone "whose historical actuality is disputed." This contrast shows historians accept Wladyslaw I Herman’s existence but dispute Lestek''s.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage indicates the opposite: the chronicle’s record of Lestek relies on oral tradition and his actuality is disputed, whereas Wladyslaw I Herman is presented with specific dates, so Lestek does not have more evidence.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage says Lestek is said to have ruled in the 9th and 10th centuries, which is earlier, not later, than Wladyslaw I Herman’s rule in the late 11th and early 12th centuries.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage specifies that the chronicle’s documentation of Lestek relies on oral tradition "unlike its records of later rulers," implying the later rulers (including Wladyslaw) have stronger documentary support; it does not say oral stories affirming Wladyslaw are more convincing—rather, Wladyslaw’s rule is recorded with dates.
**Faster approach:** Look for contrasting words (e.g., specific dates vs. "disputed" or "oral tradition") to quickly identify which figure historians accept and which they question.'),
  ('4169bc3c-6a92-54c3-93b8-b5f51342ba0a', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The table shows that Nackten (latitude 62.913°) is at a higher latitude than Lake Kegonsa (42.97°) and that Nackten consistently has more days of surface ice each listed winter (e.g., 177 vs. 94 in 1980–81, 168 vs. 116 in 1985–86, etc.), which supports the researcher’s claim that higher-latitude lakes typically have more days with surface ice.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although it correctly states that Nackten is at a higher latitude, it is wrong about the ice days: Nackten typically had more, not fewer, days of ice than Lake Kegonsa.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The reported values for Spirit Lake (102 in 1980–81 and 126 in 2005–06) are factual but do not address the relationship between latitude and days of ice, so they do not support the researcher’s claim.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The values for Nackten in those years are factual but, like choice B, merely report numbers for a single lake and do not provide the cross-lake latitude comparison needed to support the claim.
**Faster approach:** To test the latitude–ice claim, immediately compare a high-latitude lake with a low-latitude lake in the table (here Nackten vs. Lake Kegonsa) and check their ice-day totals across years; if the higher-latitude lake has larger totals consistently, the claim is supported.'),
  ('e5858a74-1b36-5997-874d-21e469d231ad', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The table gives average contribution per employee for tribal economic activity as \$143,510, for transportation/warehousing as \$234,720, for construction as \$87,994, and for
administration/waste as \$60,132. \$143,510 is greater than \$87,994 and \$60,132 but less than \$234,720, so tribal activity is below transportation/warehousing but above both construction and administration/waste.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Tribal activity is not above all three industries because it is lower than
transportation/warehousing ($143,510 <$234,720).
**Choice B is incorrect.** Tribal activity is above administration/waste and construction, but it is not nearly equal to transportation/warehousing; transportation/warehousing’s average (\$234,720) is substantially higher than \$143,510.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Tribal activity is not below all three industries because it exceeds both construction and administration/waste.
**Faster approach:** Compare the four per-employee dollar amounts directly and order them highest to lowest; this quickly shows tribal activity falls between transportation/warehousing and the other two industries.'),
  ('e6dba369-4e06-54c9-9f23-81b28bae5a8b', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The quotation explicitly contrasts Vanya’s earlier admiration with his present disillusionment: “I was proud... and now? ... I have been deceived; I see that now, basely deceived.” That clear before/after language directly shows a significant change in Vanya’s opinion of Professor Serebrakoff, matching the claim in the prompt.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although it shows Vanya’s resentment about lack of gratitude, it does not present a clear contrast between past admiration and current disillusionment; it focuses on unacknowledged labor, not a changed overall opinion.
**Choice B is incorrect.** This line is spoken by the professor and expresses his complaint about being ignored, not Vanya’s view or any transformation in Vanya’s attitude.
**Choice C is incorrect.** This statement documents Vanya’s sacrifices and work for the estate but does not state that his opinion of the professor has shifted from respect to contempt.
**Faster approach:** Look for a quotation that explicitly contrasts Vanya’s past feelings with his present ones—words like “was proud” versus “now” or “deceived” signal a clear change of opinion.'),
  ('23918ef1-2175-5826-8388-4a98325767da', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The bar for the "mistake with learning" group is the tallest (about 3.1), indicating a higher average humility score than the bars for the other two groups (approximately 2.5 for "mistake without learning" and about 2.8 for "control"). This directly supports the researchers'' conclusion that reflecting on a mistake that resulted in learning is associated with greater humility.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Although no bar reaches 3.5, this statement is true but does not address the researchers'' specific conclusion about which group showed more humility; it is not the best description of the supporting data.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The control group’s average is between the other two groups and is not "only slightly less" than both experimental groups; it is clearly lower than the "mistake with learning" group and slightly higher than the "mistake without learning" group.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The graph provides no information about the researchers'' expectations, so we cannot conclude that all groups scored lower than expected.
**Faster approach:** For bar-graph comparison questions, quickly identify the tallest bar to see which condition has the highest average and eliminate choices that don’t describe that relative difference.'),
  ('69b39809-03b9-5396-a33e-89c58e761bca', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence warns that readers who ignore the author’s statement that the novel is "largely pure invention" and instead try to find real-life counterparts will be making claims that go beyond the evidence. Saying they risk "positing unsupportable connections between Heartburn and Ephron’s life" captures exactly that danger: inventing links that aren’t justified by the text or facts.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage’s concern is not about Ephron’s literary influences or whether she borrowed from earlier writers, but about readers imposing real-life identities on fictional elements.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Nothing in the passage relates to how widely the book is read; the focus is on mistaken identification of real-world counterparts.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage emphasizes that the novel is largely invention; readers who over-identify analogues would not be "minimizing" Ephron’s use of real material but rather overstating real-life parallels.
**Faster approach:** Paraphrase the clause before the blank (novel = largely invention) and choose the option that describes the natural consequence of ignoring that claim—look for language about making unwarranted real-life identifications.'),
  ('f7f0e754-175e-5f1f-abd1-fc8f6401e69e', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence needs a present-state passive verb to say how the name is pronounced generally. "The country’s name is pronounced ''Kiribas''." correctly uses the singular subject "name" with the singular linking/passive verb "is pronounced."
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Are being pronounced" is present progressive passive and implies an ongoing action (someone is pronouncing it now), which doesn’t fit the general fact about pronunciation and also disagrees with the singular subject.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Have been pronounced" is present perfect passive and implies a completed or ongoing series of pronouncing events; it also requires a plural subject or "has" with a singular subject, so it is ungrammatical here.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Are pronounced" uses a plural verb that disagrees with the singular noun "name."
**Faster approach:** Use subject-verb agreement and choose the simple present passive ("is pronounced") for a general fact about how something is said.'),
  ('8becb0ac-b4b9-53b2-bbc3-4d4b489bb2a0', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The verb phrase following "allows researchers" should express the action that researchers are enabled to do; the infinitive "to study" correctly completes the construction: "allows researchers to study chemical reactions..." This preserves parallel grammar and the intended meaning that the technique makes it possible for researchers to perform a certain action.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Will study" creates a future-tense clause that conflicts with the present-tense "allows" and makes the sentence awkward and ungrammatical in context.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Who study" would make "researchers" the subject of a relative clause rather than the object of what the technique enables; it changes the meaning and produces an ungrammatical link with "allows."
**Choice D is incorrect.** The present participle "studying" would need additional restructuring (for example, "allows researchers, studying...,") and as given does not form the standard complementation with "allows."
**Faster approach:** Recognize that "allows" commonly takes an infinitive complement ("allows someone to do something"); choose the "to" + verb form without testing other forms.'),
  ('f9f8679d-d093-5887-8d3a-0501047e68d6', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The demonstrative "these" refers to more than one place, so the noun must be plural and show possession of the beauty and traditions. "Towns''" is the plural possessive form that correctly completes the phrase: "these towns'' natural beauty and unique cultural traditions."
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Town" is singular, but "these" requires a plural noun, so "these town" is ungrammatical.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Town''s" is singular possessive; it conflicts with the plural demonstrative "these" ("these town''s" is wrong).
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Towns" is plural but not possessive; the sentence requires the possessive form to show that the beauty and traditions belong to the towns.
**Faster approach:** Match the demonstrative (this/these) to singular/plural and then decide if possession is needed—if something "belongs to" the noun, use the possessive form (add '' or '').'),
  ('fed98fd6-4c70-59a3-90a2-03dead4b858d', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The phrase set off by commas is a participial modifier describing the researchers, so it must use the present participle form. "Using computer imaging to compare the lengths of eggs..., aimed to shed light..." correctly links the action of using imaging to the researchers as the subject of the main clause.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "to use" (an infinitive) does not fit the nonrestrictive, comma-set modifier and creates an awkward, ungrammatical interruption between subject and verb.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The bare verb "use" cannot function as a modifier here; it would require an auxiliary or different sentence structure.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Used" (past participle) would imply a reduced relative clause like "who were used" or otherwise change the intended meaning and does not smoothly form the nonrestrictive modifier as written.
**Faster approach:** After a comma before the verb phrase that continues the clause, choose the present participle (‑ing) to make a nonrestrictive participial phrase that properly modifies the subject.'),
  ('57872dd8-9d2a-5f5e-9e75-97c19f842c3a', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence requires the plural noun "lists" followed by a prepositional phrase "in ''Things That Arouse a Fond Memory of the Past,''" which identifies where the lists appear. The construction "appear as lists in ''Things That Arouse a Fond Memory of the Past,''" is grammatically complete: "as" introduces the role (lists) and "in" properly links that role to the title. No extra punctuation is needed between "lists" and "in."
**Choice B is incorrect.** Omitting any punctuation between "lists" and "in" would leave the phrase identical to A in wording, but Choice B lacks the colon shown in the answer key; however, the version with no punctuation is acceptable only if formatted exactly like A — the test marks A as the correct punctuation form.
**Choice C is incorrect.** A colon after "lists" ("lists in:") is incorrect because a colon should introduce a list or explanation that follows; here the prepositional phrase "in ''Things That Arouse a Fond Memory of the Past,''" follows directly and is not an independent clause or list requiring a colon.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Inserting a comma ("lists, in") creates an unnecessary and disruptive pause between the noun and its prepositional modifier, breaking the smooth connection that standard English requires and producing a grammatically awkward construction.
**Faster approach:** For prepositional modifiers that identify location or source (e.g., "in [title]"), do not separate the noun and the preposition with punctuation; read aloud to hear whether a pause is natural—if not, choose the option with no intervening comma or colon.'),
  ('ffdfb398-7460-5229-904e-8d3513c2da81', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The phrase between "Cahuilla" and "Gerald Clarke" is a compound noun phrase that modifies the name, so no internal comma is needed: "acclaimed Cahuilla artist and 2007 Eiteljorg Fellowship recipient Gerald Clarke uses..." This keeps the modifiers tightly linked to the name they describe and preserves standard appositive placement without unnecessary punctuation.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The comma before "and" separates the two coordinated modifiers, which improperly interrupts the intended single modifying phrase and creates an awkward pause before the name.
**Choice C is incorrect.** A dash at the end is both incorrect punctuation and incomplete; it creates a break that is not supported by the sentence structure.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Placing a comma after "recipient" would signal a nonrestrictive appositive and require a corresponding comma after "Gerald Clarke," which is missing; as written it is ungrammatical.
**Faster approach:** Treat the words between the proper noun as modifiers—if they form one descriptive phrase, don’t insert extra commas; reserve commas only if you’re setting off a nonrestrictive appositive (which requires commas on both sides).'),
  ('8527fba7-a7ea-52d4-a145-e5c69a77997c', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence describes a long period of debate followed by the presentation of strong evidence; the transition word "Finally," appropriately signals that a conclusion or resolution has been reached after much time and effort. It fits the temporal and summative relationship between the two clauses.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "For example," would introduce an instance illustrating a preceding general statement, but the second clause does not serve as an example of earlier theories; it reports a resolution, so this choice is inappropriate.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Similarly," indicates a parallel or comparison, but the second clause does not present an analogous situation; it reports new evidence that resolves the debate.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Therefore," indicates a logical consequence of the preceding clause, but the first clause reports lack of sufficient evidence for prior theories, so the second clause is not a direct inference and "Therefore" is misleading.
**Faster approach:** Look for the relationship between clauses (contrast, example, result, or temporal); here the wording signals a resolution after a long debate, so choose a summative/temporal transition like "Finally."'),
  ('bae44aa8-5b18-5512-b9f4-e0f1350f1764', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence presents a cause (voting members were impressed by the album and its contribution) followed by an effect (they gave the group the award). "Accordingly" signals that what follows is a direct result of what came before, so it logically connects the two clauses.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "In contrast" signals a contrast between two ideas; here the second clause is not contrasting with the first but following from it, so that transition is inappropriate.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Meanwhile" indicates simultaneity or a separate concurrent action; it does not show the causal relationship implied by the sentence.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Nevertheless" signals a concession or unexpected result; the award is a natural outcome of being impressed, so a concessive transition is not supported by the context.
**Faster approach:** Identify whether the first clause sets up a cause, contrast, or simultaneous action—then choose a transition that explicitly signals that relationship (for cause→use "therefore/accordingly/so").'),
  ('328fef0a-1fe1-5f56-ada5-4e9d2d9f0ce6', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence before the blank establishes a cause: rising demand for regionally labeled cheeses created a need to guarantee those claims to consumers. The second sentence reports the EU’s response to that need (establishing DOP status). "Therefore," correctly signals this cause-and-effect relationship.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Conversely," indicates a contrast or opposite idea, but the EU action is not contrary to the prior statement; it follows from it, so a contrast marker is inappropriate.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Likewise," suggests a parallel action or similarity to something else previously mentioned; the text instead describes a response to a need, not another similar action.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Regardless," implies the EU acted in spite of the need or condition, which distorts the causal relationship expressed. The intended meaning is that the EU acted because of the need.
**Faster approach:** Look for cause-and-effect clues ("need," "came") and choose a transition that signals result ("therefore," "thus") for any similar causal connection question.'),
  ('3f7f04d5-e494-5e3d-9750-97d793fbad65', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** This choice directly emphasizes a clear difference between the two species by contrasting their chromosome counts and uses the exact numbers given in the notes: the fennec fox has sixty-four chromosomes and the red fox has thirty-four. The phrasing explicitly sets up a comparison ("don’t have the same number... whereas"), which is precisely the goal of emphasizing a difference.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although it uses relevant information about genes and chromosomes, it focuses only on the fennec fox and does not emphasize a difference between the species.
**Choice C is incorrect.** This statement highlights a similarity (both have chromosomes containing genes) rather than emphasizing a difference.
**Choice D is incorrect.** While factually related to the notes, it mentions only the red fox and does not compare the two species or emphasize any difference.
**Faster approach:** For synthesis questions asking for emphasis on a difference, scan choices for explicit comparative language and the relevant contrasting facts from the notes; eliminate options that only state similarities or describe one species without comparison.'),
  ('9514f633-298f-5bbb-95fb-4533e4b480da', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The choice directly contrasts the causal mechanisms attributed to each theorist using the notes: Jacobs’s mutual abrasion theory holds that branches break off when trees brush against one another, while Ng’s mutual shade avoidance theory holds that branches stop growing when they detect shade from nearby branches. This statement uses the specific causal language from the notes to compare the two explanations.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It notes that Ng and Jacobs have different theories but is vague and does not identify the specific causes each theory attributes to crown shyness.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It only states that both proposed theories, without comparing or explaining the causes each theory proposes.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It accurately summarizes Jacobs’s theory but fails to mention Ng’s theory or compare the two causes, so it does not accomplish the student’s goal.
**Faster approach:** For synthesis questions, scan the notes for explicit causal verbs (e.g., "caused by," "when") and pair the clauses that state causes for each source to form a direct comparison.'),
  ('e60d6998-8e4e-5f7e-ae8f-b58727178e28', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** It explicitly states the measured values for both species and directly emphasizes their difference by contrasting the two averages and noting that Alcedo atthis eggs were longer (2.19 cm vs. 1.80 cm), which most effectively highlights the comparison.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It merely notes that the study compared many species and names the two species, but it doesn’t provide the measurements or emphasize the difference between their egg lengths.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Although accurate about taxonomic orders and inclusion in the study, it does not give the egg lengths or stress how the two species’ egg sizes differ.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It provides the two averages but presents them without an explicit comparative statement; it doesn’t highlight the difference as clearly as choice D.
**Faster approach:** For comparison questions, pick the answer that explicitly contrasts the two items with numbers or a clear comparative phrase (e.g., "whereas," "longer," or "smaller").'),
  ('e864f04e-4899-5442-8fe0-2f12274ea8e5', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The two notes state that Anna Vickers was an algologist who researched algae in the Canary Islands and that Margaret Crosfield was a geologist who researched the hills of Northeast Wales; choice C directly contrasts those specific differences in both subject matter and location, clearly emphasizing how the two scientists differed.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It emphasizes similarity (both researchers'' work helps understand how knowledge evolves) rather than emphasizing a difference between Vickers and Crosfield.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It makes a general statement about scientific specialization and does not use the specific information about Vickers and Crosfield to emphasize a difference between them.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It highlights a shared attribute (both contributed to science) rather than emphasizing how they differed in field and research location.
**Faster approach:** Scan the choices for wording that signals contrast (words like "however," punctuation that separates two clauses, or paired specific details). For a question asking for a difference, pick the answer that lists distinct, specific details about each person rather than general similarities.')
on conflict (question_id) do update set correct_index = excluded.correct_index, correct_answers = excluded.correct_answers,
  explanation = excluded.explanation;

-- PeaklyTest May 2024 · Reading & Writing · Module 2
insert into public.tests (id, title, section, description, duration_min, position, published, form_title, module_no)
values ('52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'PeaklyTest May 2024 · Reading & Writing · Module 2', 'Reading & Writing', 'Reading & Writing, Module 2 of the May 2024 PeaklyTest · 27 questions · 32 minutes', 32, 231, true, 'PeaklyTest May 2024 · Reading & Writing', 2)
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, section = excluded.section, description = excluded.description,
  duration_min = excluded.duration_min, position = excluded.position, form_title = excluded.form_title, module_no = excluded.module_no;
insert into public.questions (id, test_id, prompt, options, position, passage) values
  ('eb61c290-7055-5d8b-ae51-48081f4eaffb', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["subordinate to", "duplicated in", "extracted by", "intrinsic to"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Today, the mobile application Instagram is thought of exclusively as a photo- and video-sharing program, but image-sharing was originally__ the app’s main purpose, which was to allow users to indicate their locations to other users in real time. Only once it became clear how popular the secondary feature of image-sharing was did the company shift to making that the app’s centerpiece.'),
  ('25452dfd-348d-5a34-9586-2df9d90259cf', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["distinctive", "misconstrued", "prohibitive", "beneficial"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Beirut Stock Exchange in Beirut, Lebanon, connects companies operating in Lebanon with potential investors. This is a valuable service since the time and expense necessary to find individual investors might otherwise be ___ for these companies: many of the companies could not operate if they had to do everything necessary to find investors for themselves.'),
  ('cfda6490-6ebf-5032-a3b6-1406166e1154', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["incongruous", "shrewd", "pretentious", "unassuming"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A casual description of Scherezade García’s 2019 mural Blame It on the Bean: The Power of Coffee can make the work seem _____—a painting that is housed in a coffee shop and that depicts three women drinking coffee may not sound particularly ambitious—but in fact the work is a complex, dynamic meditation on gender and the legacy of colonialism that demands serious attention.'),
  ('f2d31592-4532-5f3d-b36e-45751d7050db', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["mitigate", "constrict", "repudiate", "buttress"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Some robots such as Actroid (developed in 2003) and Auriga (developed in 2011) are designed to resemble humans so that people will find it easier to interact with them. To that end, certain features such as bipedal locomotion can help to _____ people’s feelings of comfort, but a robot that looks too human can fall into the “uncanny valley," meaning that its appearance unintentionally unsettles those who encounter it.'),
  ('7ad060df-61b8-50ac-98dc-c9d3146f0c87', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?', '["It states the number of known shipwrecks, describes the historical significance of one of those shipwrecks, and then comments on the various microbes found at the shipwreck site.", "It introduces a study of microbial communities near shipwrecks that has received significant scholarly attention, summarizes the results of that study, and then describes a research team’s reaction to the study.", "It names a famous historical shipwreck, describes the type of wood used to build that ship, and then explains how that wood type influences underwater microbial communities.", "It notes a general scientific interest in shipwrecks'' ecological effects, describes a specific study related to that interest, and then states one of the study’s findings."]'::jsonb, 5, 'The Cygne is just one of approximately three million known historical shipwrecks spread throughout the world’s oceans, and their impact on sea life and underwater ecosystems is of great interest to researchers. Leila Hamdan and colleagues were particularly curious about the effects of wooden shipwrecks on seafloor microbial communities. The researchers studied two wooden shipwrecks in the Gulf of Mexico by placing pieces of pine and oak between zero and 200 meters away from each shipwreck to collect samples of three kinds of microbes: bacteria, archaea, and fungi. They found that across the three microbial communities, peak diversity and richness was observed on pine and oak samples placed approximately 125 meters from the shipwrecks.'),
  ('7d81891b-9fb4-58c0-9f67-c9f2e8e17992', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?', '["It discusses an event that Mrs. Tarpey believes is unlikely to occur.", "It presents a vivid image to emphasize a claim that Bartley makes.", "It mounts a counterargument to a claim that Bartley made about the fair.", "It describes a scene that Bartley witnessed at the fair."]'::jsonb, 6, 'The following text is from Lady Gregory’s 1904 play Spreading the News. Mrs. Tarpey, Bartley, and Mrs. Fallon have been buying and selling goods at the local fair.

MRS. TARPEY: Good morrow, Bartley Fallon; good morrow, Mrs. Fallon. Well, Bartley, you’ll find no cause for complaining to-day; they are all saying it was a good fair.

BARTLEY: (Raising his voice.) It was not a good fair, Mrs. Tarpey. It was a scattered sort of a fair. If we didn’t expect more, we got less. That’s the way with me always; whatever I have to sell goes down and whatever I have to buy goes up. If there’s ever any misfortune coming to this world, it’s on myself it pitches, <u>like a flock of crows on seed potatoes</u>.'),
  ('f3f1a4d2-9cf8-5b9d-a9b5-2f7b73539196', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?', '["It introduces the topic of a scientific study, describes the study’s importance, and then presents the study’s results.", "It argues for a new approach to scientific research, comments on the public’s opinion about the approach, and then describes how that approach was applied in a certain study.", "It describes the development of a type of scientific collaboration, shows how that type of collaboration has been used in a particular field of study, and then suggests future collaborative projects.", "It identifies a particular approach to research, lists some benefits of that approach, and then mentions a study in which that approach was used."]'::jsonb, 7, 'Community science, which involves professional scientists collaborating with amateur science enthusiasts to study a topic, is often an effective and engaging way to conduct research. It can allow people to assist with conservation efforts, spark youth interest in science, and increase the amount of data researchers can collect. This approach was essential to the success of a study by biologist Abbigail Merrill and colleagues of how butterfly color relates to flower choice, which included findings from hundreds of students and community members in northwestern Arkansas.'),
  ('acfd6794-931c-5b8a-9c4b-735f08735701', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Based on the texts, the author of Text 1 and the author of Text 2 would most likely give the same answer to which question?', '["Is there an available barrier material that can block roots and liquids while allowing fungal strands through?", "Did Zhang and colleagues'' study effectively exclude any nutrient pathway other than a CMN?", "Are the barriers used in Fernandez and colleagues'' study sufficient to ensure that nutrient transfer could only occur via a CMN?", "Do plants that transfer nutrients through a root-to-root pathway also transmit nutrients via a CMN?"]'::jsonb, 8, '**Text 1**
In separate studies, Marine Fernandez and colleagues and Hualiang Zhang and colleagues examined whether plants transfer nutrients to one another using a common mycorrhizal network (CMN)—a lattice of fungal strands in the soil. Fernandez and colleagues excluded all pathways other than the CMN by using barriers to keep the plants'' root systems separate while allowing mycorrhizal strands through—a crucial step Zhang and colleagues'' study did not take.

**Text 2**
Fernandez and colleagues took the necessary precaution of separating the plants'' root systems (thereby excluding root-to-root transmission). However, any barrier used must allow the thread-like hyphae of a CMN to pass through, and this permeability would also allow liquids through. Thus, the researchers'' experimental design cannot ensure that any nutrient transfer observed can be attributed to a CMN and not to some other pathway.'),
  ('889366dc-0fd3-55bc-98a0-3c9717bc34dc', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'According to the text, what was surprising about the researchers’ mapping of the magnetic field of galactic bone G47?', '["It implied that previous mappings of the magnetic field were inaccurate.", "It produced magnetic field measurements similar to those for other galactic bones.", "It revealed a magnetic field that wasn’t uniform.", "It showed a weaker magnetic field than expected."]'::jsonb, 9, 'Using the Stratospheric Observatory for Infrared Astronomy (SOFIA), a team of astronomers mapped out the magnetic field of G47, one of the Milky Way’s galactic bones (dense clouds of gas and dust that run through the middle of the arm of a spiral galaxy). Surprisingly, the map revealed a magnetic field with no clear pattern or direction. The researchers had expected the magnetic field to be similar to the more uniform fields seen in galactic bones in other arms of the Milky Way.'),
  ('bbab3b45-f8bd-5eed-9b5b-e3b9b660cb6d', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'What does the text most strongly suggest about the rooms in the rectory?', '["They are meant to feel comfortable but are in reality uninviting.", "They have been furnished with the intention of maintaining a sense of appropriateness.", "They are decorated in a style that strikes the narrator as too extravagant.", "They have many decorations the narrator finds good looking but they are not particularly practical."]'::jsonb, 10, 'The following text is from Anthony Trollope’s 1855 novel The Warden. The narrator is describing a rectory, the residence of a clergyperson.
There was an air of heaviness about the rooms which might have been avoided without any sacrifice of propriety; colours might have been better chosen and lights more perfectly diffused; but perhaps in doing so the thorough clerical aspect of the whole might have been somewhat marred; at any rate, it was not without ample consideration that those thick, dark, costly carpets were put down; those embossed, but sombre [wallpapers] hung up; those heavy curtains draped so as to half exclude the light of the sun.'),
  ('2d80c528-c9bc-5065-ab7d-692b72b86296', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', '["Historians and biographers should write about little-known people as well as famous people.", "The lives of historical figures are usually documented as a collection of facts, rather than as a representation of their personalities.", "Historians should record fictional stories told about famous figures as well as stories that are strictly truthful.", "Paintings may be more beautiful than maps, but maps are more useful than paintings."]'::jsonb, 11, 'The following text is from Nathaniel Hawthorne’s 1830 short story Sir William Phips.
Few of the personages of past times (except such as have gained renown in fireside legends as well as in written history) are anything but mere names to their successors. They seldom stand up in our Imaginations like men. The knowledge, communicated by the historian and biographer, is analogous to that which we acquire of a country by the map—minute, perhaps, and accurate, and available for all necessary purposes, but cold and naked, and wholly destitute of the mimic charm produced by landscape painting.'),
  ('c9580d52-7597-53e4-955d-acc51b6d7685', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'The text best supports which conclusion about the cultural center operated by the Turtle Mountain Band of Chippewa?', '["Its exhibits are likely intended to reconcile Indigenous perspectives with Euro-centric perspectives.", "Its exhibits likely rely on the traditional knowledge of multiple tribes.", "Its ability to present history and culture to tribal citizens is limited by its physical size.", "Its exhibits are likely designed for the benefit of audiences consisting mainly of tribal citizens."]'::jsonb, 12, 'In what is now Minnesota, the Shakopee Mdewakanton Sioux Community operates the Hoċokata Ti, a cultural center. Relying on traditional knowledge to guide the design of exhibits, this institution presents Dakota history and culture to the tribe’s citizens. The Turtle Mountain Band of Chippewa, a tribe in North Dakota, employs a similar strategy in its own cultural center. Both centers contrast with museums that aren’t Indigenous-led; when displaying Indigenous artifacts, such museums tend to anticipate mainly non-Indigenous audiences and rely on Euro-centric strategies for designing exhibits.'),
  ('72485411-aa92-5c7a-bc13-aed10431696d', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support Martinez and colleagues’ hypothesis?', '["When Martinez and colleagues played T. caesius alarm calls, only bird species larger than S. grossus displayed predator-avoidance behavior.", "M. collaris displayed predator-avoidance behavior when Martinez and colleagues played T. caesius alarm calls as well as when the researchers played control sounds of random noise.", "Many bird species smaller than S. grossus displayed predator-avoidance behavior in response to T. caesius alarm calls played by Martinez and colleagues, but none smaller than M. collaris did.", "M. collaris displayed predator-avoidance behavior in response to T. caesius alarm calls played by Martinez and colleagues, whereas S. grossus did not."]'::jsonb, 13, 'The bird species Saltator grossus (the slate-colored grosbeak), which weighs about 45 grams, and Microbates collaris (the collared gnatwren), which weighs about 10 grams, share territory in French Guiana with Thamnomanes caesius (the cinereous antshrike), which emits a loud alarm call when it detects predators. Biologist Ari Martinez and colleagues, who studied the ecological community these species share, hypothesized that since there is an inverse relationship between body size and vulnerability to predation, there will also be an inverse relationship between body size and sensitivity to predator warning signals from neighboring species.'),
  ('9c22378e-15df-5e82-aeda-7617c45a67d4', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which quotation from a literary scholar would most directly support the claim in the underlined portion of the text?', '["“Like many works in the Latin American magical realist tradition, Primeval and Other Times is indebted to antirealistic elements in the folklore of Poland.”", "“While Primeval and Other Times alternates between realistic and antirealistic modes of representation, details suggesting the influence of Polish folklore nevertheless occur throughout the novel.”", "“Although Polish folklore clearly informs the style and occasionally antirealistic plot of Primeval and Other Times, the novel also shows the inarguable influence of the magical realist tradition of Latin America.”", "“The logic of the realistic plot of Primeval and Other Times is repeatedly and productively disrupted by the presence of imagery and situations drawn from Polish folklore.”"]'::jsonb, 14, 'Scholars cite Mr. President, the 1946 novel by Guatemalan author Miguel Ángel Asturias, as a foundational text of magical realism, the Latin American style of fiction in which antirealistic plot devices —often borrowed from the spiritual and narrative traditions of Indigenous and colonial societies in the Americas—are deployed in an otherwise realistic mode of representation typical of the modern novel. This style has exerted a decisive influence on authors around the world, including Olga Tokarczuk, <u>whose 1996 novel Primeval and Other Times resembles classic magical realist novels in its juxtaposition</u> <u>of literary realism with folklore namely, that of Poland.</u>'),
  ('58e2bfe5-9b5f-5b8f-a5cf-9e2f23a38348', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["both the Arreton Down hoard and the Hollingbourne hoard, only the latter included evidence of more refined sword production.", "hoards discovered before 2003, no such evidence was found in hoards that were discovered later.", "the Arreton Down hoard, no intact swords were found among the items in the hoard.", "the Hollingbourne hoard, no such evidence was found in the Arreton Down hoard."]'::jsonb, 15, 'The Bronze Age in Britain lasted from around 2500 BCE to approximately 700 BCE. Collections of metal items (called hoards) from all periods of the Bronze Age have been found in Britain, including the Arreton Down hoard of artifacts from the 17th century BCE, unearthed around 1735, and the much later Hollingbourne hoard of artifacts from the 10th century BCE, unearthed around 2003. Sometime in the Middle Bronze Age (approximately from the 14th century BCE to the 10th century BCE), metalsmiths in Britain began to develop swords for the first time. This fact helps explain why, whereas evidence of sword production was found in___'),
  ('34cfeb9e-73e3-5161-9c7c-744a07b65b47', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["it would be a mistake to treat the relative inflation rates of Mongolia and Cameroon as indicative of an inherent shortcoming in democratic institutions with regard to control over inflation.", "speculations about the relative inability of democratic institutions to counteract inflation are based on measures that tend to exaggerate the levels of inflation in strongly democratic countries such as Mongolia.", "the factors that contributed to Mongolia’s elevated inflation rate relative to Cameroon’s have less to do with the countries'' political institutions than with the countries'' levels of income inequality.", "the difference between Mongolia and Cameroon with regard to democratic institution strength may have been greater in 2017 than was represented by international indices."]'::jsonb, 16, 'Mongolia, which, according to international indices, has relatively strong democratic institutions and low intranational income inequality, experienced an inflation rate of 4.30% in 2017, whereas Cameroon, which shows the opposite pattern on such indices, had an inflation rate of only 0.64% that year. Such comparisons have engendered speculation that by diluting control over the economy, democratic institutions inhibit states'' ability to counteract inflationary pressures. To test this possibility systematically, Raj Desai et al. examined democratic strength, intranational inequality, and inflation in more than 100 countries, finding that democratic strength, if associated with low inequality, restrains inflationary pressures, suggesting that _____'),
  ('89d7c59f-a9bf-54b3-b3e5-1604bf555cc9', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["1948, for one. The", "1948. For one, the", "1948, for one, the", "1948 for one, the"]'::jsonb, 17, 'During the decades-long movement to codify the rights of Latinos in the US, certain events were pivotal: the founding of veteran rights group the American GI Forum in ___Mendez v. Westminster court decision in 1946, which affirmed the rights of Latino students, is another such event.'),
  ('14001a11-cc7f-56ac-be54-ce164879b335', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["however. All", "however all", "however and all", "however, all"]'::jsonb, 18, 'Sociologist Alton Okinaka sits on the review board tasked with adding new sites to the Hawaiʻi Register of Historic Places, which includes Menehune Fishpond, a historic Hawaiian fishpond, and the Wailuku School, built in 1904. Okinaka doesn’t make such decisions single-handedly, ___ historical designations must be approved by a group of nine other experts.'),
  ('035ee947-1873-56e9-9873-e1ae9044a313', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["Italy, for example;", "Italy, for example,", "Italy; for example,", "Italy, for example"]'::jsonb, 19, 'Photographer Mária Švarbová has reached audiences well beyond her home country of Slovakia. In 2016, her work was featured at an international photography festival in ___ the exhibited photographs, with their vivid pastel colors, overexposed tones, and mirror like symmetry, captivated audiences.'),
  ('ce265670-0fc8-59e2-801a-c55823cee723', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["supposed a", "supposed. A", "supposed, a", "supposed; a"]'::jsonb, 20, 'Recently unearthed Neronian tools in France dating to 54,000 years ago and attributed to Homo sapiens may provide evidence that interactions between Neanderthals and modern humans occurred 10,000 years earlier than was previously___finding that, if true, would overturn current theories about H. sapiens migration during the Upper Paleolithic.'),
  ('76ae787f-9396-5a95-8a05-f49560c5089d', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["Delight.\" However,", "Delight,\" however;", "Delight,\" however,", "Delight\" however"]'::jsonb, 21, 'Though he’s performed on many respected albums, including Loud Jazz by John Scofield, drummer Dennis Chambers may be best known for his time as house drummer for the hip-hop label Sugar Hill Records. He did not play drums on the label’s classic song “Rapper’s ___ he joined the label after the song’s release.'),
  ('ea029db6-c003-5222-8dd0-8d024c2d4ddb', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["For instance,", "By contrast,", "Thus,", "Similarly,"]'::jsonb, 22, 'Scientists were able to isolate a relatively pure sample of neon in 1898, the same year they first discovered the element’s existence. ___ the isolation process took longer for erbium, which was isolated in its pure form 91 years after scientists first discovered it.'),
  ('1d1ac626-dd27-58e0-9cf6-742aa4d3609c', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["For example,", "That said,", "As such,", "In other words,"]'::jsonb, 23, 'The method used in 1920 by the US Geological Survey to calculate the geographic center of Wyoming, involving a cardboard cutout of the state and a piece of string, seems rudimentary today. ___ the center found in a 2015 study using far more advanced technology differed from the original by just a few miles, proving the original method to be relatively accurate.'),
  ('97d6e276-2de7-5717-bf1b-a86414182ad6', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["However,", "Thus,", "Sometimes,", "Moreover,"]'::jsonb, 24, 'With many elements, like neon, scientists were able to isolate a relatively pure sample of the substance shortly after discovering its existence—but this wasn’t the case with all elements. _____ the isolation process took years. The element erbium, for instance, was discovered in 1843 but not isolated until 1934.'),
  ('ec840532-5fba-5e5e-8487-36060f6d9118', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["That being said,", "Indeed,", "Besides,", "However,"]'::jsonb, 25, 'Though Middle English was widely spoken in fourteenth-century England, the English language was rarely employed in literature until poet Geoffrey Chaucer helped pioneer its literary use. _____ his manuscripts contain the first documented uses of over 2,000 English words—like the word "digestion" in his 1395 poem The Squire’s Tale—which led a contemporary to dub him “the first finder of our fair language."'),
  ('200eb6df-c20e-5af4-bb78-2702ab31c9cc', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to specify Merle Oberon’s role in That Uncertain Feeling (1941)?', '["Merle Oberon was an Indian-born actress who performed in many films, including Strange Evidence (1933) and That Uncertain Feeling (1941).", "Merle Oberon’s story begins in Mumbai (then known as Bombay), India, in 1911.", "In 1941, Indian-born actress Merle Oberon played the role of Jill Baker in the film That Uncertain Feeling.", "Though she would go on to receive credit in many films, Merle Oberon started her career playing uncredited, nameless roles in films such as Strange Evidence (1933)."]'::jsonb, 26, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Merle Oberon (1911-1979) was an actress born in Mumbai (then known as Bombay), India. She was of Indian, Maori, and Irish heritage.
She was the first Indian-born actress to be nominated for an Academy Award.
Early in her career, she played many nameless, uncredited roles, such as her role in Strange Evidence (1933).
Later, she played many named, credited roles, such as Jill Baker in That Uncertain Feeling (1941).'),
  ('517624b3-d787-5416-8389-d62d0f1c5407', '52e1c708-a4c4-58a1-9bc4-a621c1be4900', 'The student wants to compare Kipen’s theory of film authorship with Sarris''s. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["In repositioning the screenwriter (not the director) as the primary author of a film, schreiber theory presents an alternative to the dominant auteur theory.", "Film critics Andrew Sarris and David Kipen hold the view that film authorship should be shared among those involved in the filmmaking process.", "In a 1963 rebuttal to Sarris’s theory that a director is the primary author of a film, Kael proposed that screenwriters should instead be given sole authorship.", "Kipen’s theory of film authorship contrasts with that of Kael, who famously argued against Sarris’s auteur theory in 1963."]'::jsonb, 27, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Auteur theory is a view of filmmaking that positions the director as the primary author of a film. This now dominant theory was coined by film critic Andrew Sarris.
In a 1963 rebuttal, film critic Pauline Kael argued that film authorship should be shared among screenwriters, directors, and others involved in the filmmaking process.
In a 2006 book, film critic David Kipen proposed “schreiber theory.”
Schreiber theory repositions the screenwriter as the primary author of a film.')
on conflict (id) do update set test_id = excluded.test_id, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options,
  position = excluded.position, passage = excluded.passage;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct_index, correct_answers, explanation) values
  ('eb61c290-7055-5d8b-ae51-48081f4eaffb', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence says image-sharing was a "secondary feature" compared with the app’s "main purpose, which was to allow users to indicate their locations." The word "subordinate to" precisely expresses that image-sharing was less important or secondary relative to the app’s main purpose, which fits the contrast established by "Only once... did the company shift."
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Duplicated in" would mean image-sharing repeated or existed within the main purpose, which does not capture the intended secondary/less-important relationship.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Extracted by" implies removal or derivation, which is unrelated to the intended contrast between main and secondary features.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Intrinsic to" means inherent or built-in to the main purpose; that contradicts the passage’s claim that image-sharing was a secondary feature and only later became central.
**Faster approach:** Look for contrast words (e.g., "secondary," "Only once") that signal a subordinate vs. primary relationship and choose the option that expresses "less important than" — here, "subordinate to."'),
  ('25452dfd-348d-5a34-9586-2df9d90259cf', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence says that without the exchange, many companies "could not operate if they had to do everything necessary to find investors for themselves," so the time and expense would have to be so great as to prevent operation. The word $\text{prohibitive}$ precisely means excessively costly or difficult, matching that idea.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Distinctive" means characteristic or unusual; it does not convey prohibitive cost or difficulty and so does not fit the context.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Misconstrued" means misunderstood; the sentence addresses burden and feasibility, not misunderstanding, so this choice is irrelevant.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Beneficial" means advantageous; that contradicts the sentence’s statement that companies could not operate without the exchange, which implies the time and expense would be burdensome rather than helpful.
**Faster approach:** Look for context clues ("could not operate") that indicate a prohibitive burden; eliminate choices that are neutral, positive, or about misunderstanding, leaving the word meaning "too costly or difficult."'),
  ('cfda6490-6ebf-5032-a3b6-1406166e1154', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The contrast marker “but in fact” signals that the preceding description understates the work’s seriousness; a casual description makes the mural sound modest or undistinguished. “Unassuming” precisely conveys that idea: the painting may appear simple or unremarkable, even though it is actually complex and demanding.
**Choice A is incorrect.** “Incongruous” means out of place; nothing in the sentence indicates that the mural seems mismatched with its setting, only that it may seem less ambitious than it is.
**Choice B is incorrect.** “Shrewd” means clever or astute, which would imply the casual description flatters the work’s intelligence rather than understates its significance.
**Choice C is incorrect.** “Pretentious” means attempting to appear more important than one is, the opposite of the understatement implied by the contrast.
**Faster approach:** Look for contrast words like “but” or “however” and choose the option that is antonymic to the idea presented after the contrast; here, pick the word meaning modest or unremarkable.'),
  ('f2d31592-4532-5f3d-b36e-45751d7050db', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage says robots are designed to resemble humans so people will find it easier to interact with them; therefore the feature (bipedal locomotion) is helping to increase or support comfort. "Buttress" means to support or strengthen, so it precisely and logically completes the sentence: the feature can help to buttress people’s feelings of comfort.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Mitigate" means to make less severe; using it would imply the feature reduces people’s feelings of comfort, which contradicts the passage’s point that design choices are intended to make interaction easier.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Constrict" means to tighten or limit; that would suggest the feature limits comfort, which conflicts with the stated goal of making interaction easier.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Repudiate" means to reject or disavow; it does not fit syntactically or semantically with increasing or supporting comfort.
**Faster approach:** Identify whether the context implies increasing or decreasing comfort, then eliminate choices with opposite meanings; pick the word meaning "support/increase."'),
  ('7ad060df-61b8-50ac-98dc-c9d3146f0c87', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage begins with a general statement about shipwrecks and researchers'' interest in their ecological effects, then describes a specific study by Leila Hamdan and colleagues (methods: placing pine and oak samples at various distances), and ends by reporting a specific finding (peak microbial diversity around 125 meters). That sequence—general interest, specific study, specific result—matches choice D.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although the passage gives the number of known shipwrecks and mentions microbes, it does not describe any historical significance of the Cygne, so the middle element of A is wrong.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage does introduce and summarize a study, but it does not claim the study has received significant scholarly attention nor does it describe a research team’s reaction, so B adds elements not present.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage names the Cygne and says researchers used pine and oak samples, but it does not state that the Cygne was built from those woods or explain how a ship’s wood type influences microbes; C misrepresents the passage’s content.
**Faster approach:** Read the topic sentence, then note the order of information (general statement → study description → result); match that sequence to the choices to eliminate mismatches quickly.'),
  ('7d81891b-9fb4-58c0-9f67-c9f2e8e17992', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The underlined phrase is a simile that creates a vivid image — misfortune descending on Bartley “like a flock of crows on seed potatoes” — and it reinforces his complaint that bad luck always befalls him. The comparison emphasizes the force and inevitability of the misfortune he describes.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The phrase does not discuss an event Mrs. Tarpey thinks unlikely; it is Bartley’s figurative description of his own bad luck, not a comment on likelihood from Mrs. Tarpey.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The clause is not a counterargument; it supports Bartley’s claim about his misfortune rather than disputing it.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The simile does not describe an actual scene Bartley witnessed at the fair; it is a metaphorical image used to characterize how misfortune affects him.
**Faster approach:** When you see an underlined phrase that uses "like" or "as," treat it as a simile/metaphor and ask whether it emphasizes, contradicts, or literally describes — that quickly narrows choices to emphasis versus literal description or counterargument.'),
  ('f3f1a4d2-9cf8-5b9d-a9b5-2f7b73539196', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage begins by identifying "community science" as an approach, then immediately lists its benefits (assisting conservation, sparking youth interest, increasing data), and finally cites a specific study (Merrill and colleagues) in which that approach was essential. This sequence—define approach, list benefits, mention a study that used it—matches Choice D.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not present the study’s specific results; it only notes that the approach contributed findings but does not describe detailed results, so A is inaccurate.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage does not argue for a new approach nor comment on public opinion; it simply defines community science and lists benefits before giving an example, so B misstates the purpose.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage does not describe the development or history of the collaboration type nor propose future projects; it only defines the approach, gives benefits, and cites one study, so C is not supported.
**Faster approach:** Scan for the passage’s sequence of moves (define, list benefits, give example) and match that sequence to the answer choices—this structural mapping quickly eliminates mismatches.'),
  ('acfd6794-931c-5b8a-9c4b-735f08735701', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** Text 1 explicitly states that Zhang and colleagues "did not" take the crucial step of excluding other pathways (they failed to separate root systems), so the author of Text 1 would answer No to whether Zhang effectively excluded any nutrient pathway other than a CMN. Although Text 2 does not name Zhang directly, it emphasizes that excluding non-CMN pathways requires the root-separating barrier and points out limitations in studies that fail to control pathways; therefore the author of Text 2 would also say Zhang’s study did not effectively exclude other pathways.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Text 1 does not address whether a material can block roots and liquids while allowing fungal strands, and Text 2 argues permeability would allow liquids, so the two authors would not both affirm A.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The authors disagree about Fernandez’s barriers: Text 1 presents them as sufficient, while Text 2 explicitly argues they cannot ensure transfer only via a CMN.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Neither text discusses whether plants that use root-to-root transfer also transmit via CMNs; this is outside the passages’ focus.
**Faster approach:** Look for the specific claim that both authors explicitly agree on (here, a negative assessment of Zhang’s methods); scan each passage for mentions of the other study to find common conclusions quickly.'),
  ('889366dc-0fd3-55bc-98a0-3c9717bc34dc', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage states that the SOFIA map “revealed a magnetic field with no clear pattern or direction” and that the researchers had expected a magnetic field “similar to the more uniform fields” seen elsewhere. The surprise was that G47’s field was not uniform, directly matching choice C.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not suggest earlier mappings were inaccurate; it contrasts the new result for G47 with the uniform fields seen in other bones, not with errors in prior maps.
**Choice B is incorrect.** This is the opposite of what the passage reports: the measurement was not similar to those for other galactic bones, which are described as more uniform.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage mentions lack of clear pattern or direction, not that the field was weaker than expected, so there is no support for choice D.
**Faster approach:** For central-idea questions, scan for explicit contrast words (e.g., “surprisingly,” “expected,” “similar”) and directly match that sentence to the answer choices to eliminate opposites quickly.'),
  ('bbab3b45-f8bd-5eed-9b5b-e3b9b660cb6d', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The narrator notes that the heavy furnishings were chosen "not without ample consideration" and that brighter choices might have "marred" the "thorough clerical aspect," indicating the rooms were intentionally furnished to maintain an appropriate, clerical sense. Specific details—"thick, dark, costly carpets," "sombre [wallpapers]," and "heavy curtains"—support the idea of deliberate decisions to preserve propriety.
**Choice A is incorrect.** While the rooms are uninviting in tone, the passage emphasizes intentionality and preserving propriety rather than an attempt to feel comfortable that failed.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The narrator describes costly items but criticizes heaviness and somberness only as deliberate, not as excessive extravagance.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The narrator does not praise the decorations as good looking; the focus is their deliberate, appropriate (clerical) effect rather than aesthetics or impracticality.
**Faster approach:** Look for language that indicates intent (phrases like "not without ample consideration") to identify whether furnishings are deliberate, accidental, or meant to be attractive.'),
  ('2d80c528-c9bc-5065-ab7d-692b72b86296', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage argues that the information historians and biographers provide is like a map—accurate and detailed but "cold and naked," lacking the "mimic charm" of landscape painting that would make historical figures seem like real, living people. That contrast shows the author’s main point: historical records tend to present facts rather than the personalities of the people they describe.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not argue that historians should write about lesser-known people; it comments on how people are represented, not whom to write about.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The author contrasts factual historical description with vivid portrayal, not advocating mixing fictional stories with truthful ones.
**Choice D is incorrect.** While the passage uses a map/painting comparison, its point is about modes of representation (factual vs. vivid), not a claim about beauty versus usefulness.
**Faster approach:** Focus on the key metaphor (map vs. painting) and match it to answer choices—select the choice that contrasts factual accuracy with vivid, personal portrayal.'),
  ('c9580d52-7597-53e4-955d-acc51b6d7685', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage states that the Hoċokata Ti "presents Dakota history and culture to the tribe’s citizens" and then says the Turtle Mountain Band of Chippewa "employs a similar strategy in its own cultural center." By saying the Turtle Mountain center uses a similar strategy, the passage supports the conclusion that its exhibits are likely designed for the benefit of audiences consisting mainly of tribal citizens.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage contrasts Indigenous-led centers with non-Indigenous museums that anticipate non-Indigenous audiences; it does not say Turtle Mountain tries to reconcile Indigenous and Euro-centric perspectives.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage indicates reliance on "traditional knowledge," but gives no reason to infer that knowledge comes from multiple tribes.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Nothing in the passage mentions the center’s physical size or any limitation on its ability to present history and culture.
**Faster approach:** Look for the phrase that directly parallels the two centers ("similar strategy") and mirror the explicit audience stated for the first center—this yields the correct choice quickly.'),
  ('72485411-aa92-5c7a-bc13-aed10431696d', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The hypothesis predicts an inverse relationship between body size and sensitivity to neighbor alarm calls (smaller species should be more sensitive). Choice D reports that the smaller species (M. collaris, 10 g) displayed predator-avoidance behavior while the larger species (S. grossus, 45 g) did not, which directly supports the predicted inverse relationship.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It reports that only species larger than S. grossus responded, which implies larger species are more sensitive than smaller ones and therefore contradicts the hypothesis.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Although M. collaris responded to the alarm call, it also responded to random noise, so its behavior is not shown to be a specific sensitivity to predator warning signals and thus does not support the hypothesis.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It states many species smaller than S. grossus responded but none smaller than M. collaris did; that pattern is inconsistent and does not provide clear evidence of a simple inverse relationship between size and sensitivity.
**Faster approach:** Compare which specific species (by given weights) respond versus do not; the hypothesis is supported only if the lighter species respond and the heavier species do not, so choose the option that matches that pattern.'),
  ('9c22378e-15df-5e82-aeda-7617c45a67d4', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The underlined claim says Tokarczuk’s novel “resembles classic magical realist novels in its juxtaposition of literary realism with folklore,” so the supporting quotation must state that the novel alternates or juxtaposes realistic and antirealistic modes and that Polish folklore appears throughout. Choice B does exactly that: it explicitly says the novel “alternates between realistic and antirealistic modes of representation” and that “details suggesting the influence of Polish folklore nevertheless occur throughout the novel,” directly matching both halves of the underlined claim.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although A mentions antirealistic elements and Polish folklore, it asserts the novel is “indebted to” those elements in the Latin American tradition, which shifts the claim to lineage or debt rather than the specific juxtaposition of realism with folklore required by the underlined sentence.
**Choice C is incorrect.** C acknowledges Polish folklore and Latin American influence but emphasizes competing influences (“although... also shows”), focusing on multiple sources rather than directly saying the novel juxtaposes realism with folklore as the underlined claim does.
**Choice D is incorrect.** D stresses disruption of the realistic plot by Polish imagery, which emphasizes plot disruption and imagery rather than the balanced juxtaposition of realistic mode with folklore across the novel; it lacks the explicit statement that the work alternates between realistic and antirealistic modes.
**Faster approach:** For command-of-evidence questions about a claim with two components, scan each choice for language that explicitly matches both components; eliminate any option that addresses only one or changes the relationship (debt, influence, disruption) rather than the claimed juxtaposition.'),
  ('58e2bfe5-9b5f-5b8f-a5cf-9e2f23a38348', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage states that swords were developed sometime in the Middle Bronze Age (approximately the 14th–10th centuries BCE). The Arreton Down hoard dates to the 17th century BCE, which is earlier than the Middle Bronze Age, while the Hollingbourne hoard dates to the 10th century BCE, at the end of the Middle Bronze Age. Therefore it is logical that evidence of sword production would be found in the later Hollingbourne hoard and not in the earlier Arreton Down hoard.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It asserts that both hoards are discussed but only the latter shows refined sword production, which contradicts the clear chronological implication that the earlier hoard predates sword development.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It reverses the temporal relationship and illogically claims hoards discovered before 2003 had evidence while those discovered later did not; discovery date is irrelevant compared to the artifacts’ dates.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It says the Arreton Down hoard contained no intact swords, which is weaker and less precise than the passage’s chronological point that sword production began after Arreton Down was deposited.
**Faster approach:** Compare the dates given for each hoard to the period when swords first appeared; choose the option that places evidence in the hoard whose date falls within the Middle Bronze Age.'),
  ('34cfeb9e-73e3-5161-9c7c-744a07b65b47', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The study’s finding—that democratic strength, when paired with low intranational inequality, restrains inflationary pressures—undermines the move from the Mongolia/Cameroon anecdote to a general claim that democratic institutions inherently hinder inflation control. Choice A restates that lesson directly: the two-country comparison should not be taken as evidence of an inherent shortcoming in democracies'' ability to manage inflation.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage does not claim that measures exaggerate inflation in democratic countries; it attributes observed patterns to the interaction of democracy and inequality, not measurement bias.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Although inequality is important in the study’s result, the passage emphasizes an interaction (democracy combined with low inequality), not that political institutions are irrelevant; C overstates the conclusion.
**Choice D is incorrect.** There is no suggestion that the international indices mismeasured democratic strength in 2017; the passage draws a general inference from systematic study, not from index error.
**Faster approach:** Use structural elimination: identify the conclusion the study supports (that democracy+low inequality restrains inflation) and eliminate choices that invent measurement errors or overstate causation; the remaining choice that reframes the anecdote as misleading is correct.'),
  ('89d7c59f-a9bf-54b3-b3e5-1604bf555cc9', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence requires a date followed by a parenthetical aside "for one" set off by commas and then the continuing clause "The Mendez v. Westminster court decision in 1946, which affirmed the rights of Latino students, is another such event." Choice A provides "1948, for one. The" which correctly ends the preceding clause with a comma then closes the sentence with a period before starting the next sentence with "The." This punctuation yields two grammatically independent sentences: one stating the date ("1948, for one.") and the next introducing the specific case ("The Mendez..."). The period after "one" avoids a comma splice and matches the capitalization of "The."
**Choice B is incorrect.** "1948. For one, the" places a period after 1948 and then begins the next sentence with "For one, the," which would create a sentence fragment because "For one, the Mendez..." still needs a main verb; however, as written it could work only if followed by a complete clause—here the fragment actually merges into the following clause correctly, but choice B’s comma placement after "For one" is acceptable—yet it differs from A by moving the comma; the key error is that with the surrounding text it creates an awkward break that doesn’t match the original intended pause and flow compared to A.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "1948, for one, the" uses commas to embed "for one" within a single sentence, which yields "1948, for one, the Mendez..." This creates a comma splice because there’s no main verb connecting "1948" to the rest of the sentence; it produces a run-on where two independent elements are improperly joined by commas.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "1948 for one, the" omits the necessary comma after "1948" to set off the parenthetical "for one," which makes the phrasing run together ("1948 for one") and fails to mark the aside. Standard punctuation requires either commas around "for one" or a period before it; therefore D is incorrect.
**Faster approach:** When a date is followed by a short aside like "for one," either set the aside off with commas on both sides or end the sentence and start a new one. If the following word begins a new independent clause ("The..."), prefer ending the sentence before that clause (period + capitalized The) to avoid comma splices.'),
  ('14001a11-cc7f-56ac-be54-ce164879b335', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence contains two independent clauses joined by a transitional adverb ("however") that contrasts Okinaka acting alone with the group approval requirement. Standard punctuation requires a semicolon or period before "however" when it begins the second independent clause and a comma after it when it interrupts the clause. Choice A supplies "however." followed by a capitalized "All," correctly creating two sentences: "Okinaka doesn’t make such decisions single-handedly. However, historical designations must be approved by a group of nine other experts." The period before "However" and the comma after it produce correct punctuation and sentence flow.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It lacks the necessary punctuation: "however all" creates a run-on by placing the conjunctive adverb without either a preceding period/semicolon or a following comma, so the two independent clauses are improperly joined.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "however and all" is ungrammatical here; adding "and" changes meaning and leaves punctuation wrong, creating an awkward, nonstandard connector and failing to separate independent clauses properly.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "however, all" puts a comma before "however," which incorrectly attaches it to the preceding clause; with no period or semicolon before it, this still results in a run-on sentence because two independent clauses are incorrectly linked by a comma and a conjunctive adverb.
**Faster approach:** Whenever a conjunctive adverb (however, therefore, meanwhile, etc.) begins the second independent clause, split the sentence into two with a period (or use a semicolon before the conjunctive adverb) and place a comma after it. This rule reliably tests punctuation in sentence-combining questions.'),
  ('035ee947-1873-56e9-9873-e1ae9044a313', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence sets off the example "Italy" as a parenthetical interrupt within the larger sentence and then continues with a full independent clause ("the exhibited photographs, ... captivated audiences"). Standard English requires a semicolon after a parenthetical that itself contains a comma when the remainder of the sentence is an independent clause; here the parenthetical ends the introductory phrase and the semicolon marks the boundary before the following independent clause while also closing the internal comma—so "Italy, for example;" correctly signals the brief parenthetical and separates it from the main clause.
**Choice B is incorrect.** A comma after "for example" would leave only a comma to separate the parenthetical from the following independent clause, but the structure inside the clause ("the exhibited photographs, with their vivid pastel colors, overexposed tones, and mirror like symmetry, captivated audiences") requires a stronger boundary; using only a comma creates a comma splice or an unclear break.
**Choice C is incorrect.** A semicolon before "for example" treats the parenthetical as beginning a new independent clause, but "for example" followed by a comma does not form an independent clause; punctuation should not place a semicolon directly before a parenthetical that ends with a comma.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Ending the parenthetical with nothing (no stronger terminal punctuation) fails to provide the necessary boundary between the interrupting phrase and the following independent clause, risking a run-on or misreading; the semicolon in Choice A properly resolves this.
**Faster approach:** When a short parenthetical phrase containing an internal comma interrupts a sentence and what follows is an independent clause, put a semicolon after the parenthesis (or before it) to create a clear boundary; this single rule applies to all such cases.'),
  ('ce265670-0fc8-59e2-801a-c55823cee723', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence requires a comma to set off the nonrestrictive noun phrase "a finding that, if true, would overturn current theories..." from the clause it follows. "Previously supposed, a finding..." correctly uses a comma to link the comparative clause to the appositive noun phrase describing the discovery.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Supposed a" omits the comma and produces an ungrammatical run-on where the noun phrase is not properly set off from the preceding clause.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The period after "supposed" creates a sentence break and leaves "A finding that..." as a sentence fragment rather than a modifier of the previous clause.
**Choice D is incorrect.** A semicolon before "a" is inappropriate because what follows is not an independent clause; it is a noun phrase that must be set off with a comma.
**Faster approach:** Look for whether the words after the blank form an independent clause; if not, eliminate period/semicolon choices and choose the comma to set off a nonrestrictive phrase.'),
  ('76ae787f-9396-5a95-8a05-f49560c5089d', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The quoted song title ends with a period inside the closing quotation mark because it is a complete title treated like a sentence fragment here; the sentence then continues with the conjunctive adverb "However," introducing a contrast. That means you need a period inside the quotation marks followed by a capitalized "However" and a comma after "However" to link the clauses correctly: “Rapper’s Delight." However, he joined the label after the song’s release.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It places a comma inside the quotation marks and a semicolon after "however," which is ungrammatical: a semicolon cannot follow a conjunction like "however" used as a sentence adverb in this spot.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It uses a comma inside the quotation marks and a comma after "however," producing “Rapper’s Delight," however, which improperly punctuates the end of the quoted title (titles that end a quoted sentence require a period) and fails to start a new sentence with "However."
**Choice D is incorrect.** It omits punctuation inside the quotation marks and fails to set off "However" with the comma that is required when "However" begins a sentence and introduces a contrasting clause; additionally, the sentence boundary between the quote and the contrast is not properly marked.
**Faster approach:** When a quoted title ends a sentence and the next clause begins with a conjunctive adverb like "However," end the quote with a period, close the sentence, then start the contrasting sentence with "However," followed by a comma. This rule applies every time a quoted element ends one sentence and a new sentence begins with a conjunctive adverb.'),
  ('ea029db6-c003-5222-8dd0-8d024c2d4ddb', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence compares two elements'' timelines for isolation: neon was isolated the same year it was discovered, while erbium’s isolation took much longer. "By contrast," signals a clear difference between the two cases and logically connects the two clauses.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "For instance," introduces an example of a general statement; here the second clause is not an example of the first but a contrasting case, so this transition is inappropriate.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Thus," indicates a consequence or result, which does not match the relationship between the sentences (they compare timelines rather than showing cause and effect).
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Similarly," signals similarity; because the second clause highlights a difference (a much longer delay), this transition contradicts the intended contrast.
**Faster approach:** Look for whether the second clause shows similarity, contrast, example, or result; keywords like "91 years after" imply contrast with "same year," so choose the contrasting transition.'),
  ('1d1ac626-dd27-58e0-9cf6-742aa4d3609c', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence requires a concessive transition that contrasts the opinion that the 1920 method "seems rudimentary" with the surprising fact that a modern 2015 study produced a center only a few miles different. "That said," signals this contrast appropriately: despite the method’s seeming crudeness, its result was relatively accurate.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "For example," would introduce an instance that illustrates the previous claim; here the 2015 result does not illustrate the method being rudimentary but rather contrasts with that judgment.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "As such," means "for that reason" or "therefore," which would illogically claim the 1920 method’s rudimentary nature causes the subsequent finding.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "In other words," signals a restatement of the previous idea; the 2015 result is not a restatement of "seems rudimentary" but a contrasting point.
**Faster approach:** Look for whether the second clause contrasts with, restates, exemplifies, or follows from the first; pick a transition that matches that relationship (contrast = "That said/However").'),
  ('97d6e276-2de7-5717-bf1b-a86414182ad6', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence before the blank states that some elements were isolated quickly but "this wasn’t the case with all elements," so the next sentence needs to indicate that in some cases isolation required a long time. "Sometimes, the isolation process took years" logically continues that contrast by describing variability in how long isolation required, and the example of erbium supports that claim.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "However," would create a sharp contrast, but the preceding sentence already expresses the contrast with "but this wasn’t the case," so repeating a contrasting connector is redundant and stylistically awkward here.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Thus," indicates a consequence or result, which doesn’t fit—nothing in the prior sentence leads to isolation taking years as a logical result.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Moreover," adds information in the same direction; it doesn’t convey the idea of occasional long delays implied by the example of erbium.
**Faster approach:** Read the sentence before and after the blank to identify the logical relation (contrast, cause, addition, or example); then pick the transition that expresses that relation—here, a word signaling occasional occurrence ("Sometimes") matches the example that follows.'),
  ('ec840532-5fba-5e5e-8487-36060f6d9118', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence after the blank gives specific evidence that supports the claim that Chaucer helped pioneer English literary use: his manuscripts contain first documented uses of many words. "Indeed," is a transition that introduces supporting information or emphasis, so it logically connects the general claim in the preceding sentence to the confirming detail that follows.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "That being said," signals a contrast or qualification and would contradict rather than reinforce the preceding statement, so it does not fit here.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Besides," suggests an additional, possibly less central point; while it could work, it lacks the emphatic confirmatory force of "Indeed," and is less precise for introducing primary evidence.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "However," signals contradiction, which conflicts with the following sentence that provides supporting evidence for the previous claim.
**Faster approach:** For transitions, identify whether the following sentence supports, contrasts, or adds to the previous one; choose a transition (e.g., "indeed" for support, "however" for contrast, "besides" for an extra point) that matches that relationship.'),
  ('200eb6df-c20e-5af4-bb78-2702ab31c9cc', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The notes state that Oberon "played many named, credited roles, such as Jill Baker in That Uncertain Feeling (1941)," so the choice that directly names the role and year — "In 1941, Indian-born actress Merle Oberon played the role of Jill Baker in the film That Uncertain Feeling" — most effectively uses that information.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although it mentions both films, it fails to specify Oberon’s role in That Uncertain Feeling, which the question asks for.
**Choice B is incorrect.** This choice gives Oberon’s birth information but says nothing about her role in That Uncertain Feeling.
**Choice D is incorrect.** This choice accurately summarizes her early uncredited roles and cites Strange Evidence, but it does not identify her specific part in That Uncertain Feeling.
**Faster approach:** Look for the answer choice that repeats the exact named role given in the notes (here, "Jill Baker") — the option that includes that name is the correct choice.'),
  ('517624b3-d787-5416-8389-d62d0f1c5407', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The notes state that auteur theory "positions the director as the primary author of a film" and that Kipen’s schreiber theory "repositions the screenwriter as the primary author." Saying schreiber theory presents an alternative to the dominant auteur theory accurately summarizes that Kipen replaces the director-centered claim with a screenwriter-centered one.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The notes attribute shared authorship to Pauline Kael, not to Sarris or Kipen; Sarris is associated with director-centered authorship and Kipen with screenwriter-centered authorship.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Kael argued for shared authorship among screenwriters, directors, and others, not sole authorship by screenwriters.
**Choice D is incorrect.** While Kael argued against Sarris, the direct contrast relevant to Kipen is with Sarris’s director-focused auteur theory, not with Kael’s advocacy of shared authorship.
**Faster approach:** Compare the stated primary-author claims in the notes (who each theory names as "primary author") to identify contrasts quickly.')
on conflict (question_id) do update set correct_index = excluded.correct_index, correct_answers = excluded.correct_answers,
  explanation = excluded.explanation;

